{"cuisine/romanian-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/romanian-cuisine","n":"Romanian cuisine","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Romania","intro":[["Romanian cuisine is the traditional cuisine of Romania, shaped by the country's varied geography, pastoral traditions, and agricultural heritage. Rooted in the landscapes of the Carpathian Mountains, it combines locally developed dishes with culinary influences acquired through centuries of cultural contact. Its foundations reflect the culinary practices of the region since antiquity, including the legacy of the Roman period, with influences from Balkan, Central European, Ottoman and French cuisines."],["Romanian cuisine includes numerous holiday dishes arranged according to the mentioned season and holiday since the country has its religious roots in Eastern Orthodoxy. Romanian dishes consist of vegetables, cereals, fruits, honey, milk, dairy products, meat and game."],["Various kinds of dishes are available, which are sometimes included under a generic term; for example, the category ciorbă includes a wide range of soups with a characteristic sour taste. Variations include meat and vegetable soup, tripe (ciorbă de burtă) and calf foot soup, or fish soup, all of which are soured by lemon juice, sauerkraut juice (zeamă de varză), vinegar, or borș (traditionally made from bran). The category țuică (plum brandy) is a name for a strong alcoholic spirit in Romania."],["With the cuisine of Romania being shared by another country, namely Moldova, there are similarities between the cuisines of the two Romanian-speaking countries."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"romanian-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Romanian_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Dishes","count":9,"links":[["Borscht","dish/borscht"],["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["Frigărui","dish/frigarui"],["Ostropel","dish/ostropel"],["Papanași","dish/papanasi"],["Sarmale","dish/sarmale"],["Scorcolga","dish/scorcolga"],["Stuffed peppers","dish/stuffed-peppers"],["Taramasalata","dish/taramasalata"]]},{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Notes from Irina's Kitchen","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"irinageorgescu.substack.com","note":"Newsletter of Romanian recipes and food stories from cookbook author Irina Georgescu.","url":"https://irinageorgescu.substack.com/about","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Carpathia: Food from the Heart of Romania","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Irina Georgescu, 2020","note":"Romanian-born author's survey of traditional Romanian home cooking.","url":"https://www.quarto.com/books/9780711241824/carpathia","suggested":true,"isbn":"9780711241824"}]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Cabbage","ingredient/cabbage"],["Ground meat","ingredient/ground-meat"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Beetroot","ingredient/beetroot"],["Beta vulgaris","ingredient/beta-vulgaris"],["Brassica oleracea","ingredient/brassica-oleracea"],["Carrot","ingredient/carrot"],["Potato","ingredient/potato"],["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"],["Lamb","ingredient/lamb"],["Rabbit meat","ingredient/rabbit-meat"],["Scallion","ingredient/scallion"],["Tomato sauce","ingredient/tomato-sauce"],["Cheese","ingredient/cheese"],["Grape leaves","ingredient/grape-leaves"],["Walnut","ingredient/walnut"],["Bell pepper","ingredient/bell-pepper"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Bread crumbs","ingredient/bread-crumbs"],["Carp","ingredient/carp"],["Cod","ingredient/cod"],["Mashed potato","ingredient/mashed-potato"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Within Romanian cooking","count":2,"links":[["Moldovan cuisine","cuisine/moldovan-cuisine"],["Transylvanian Saxon cuisine","cuisine/transylvanian-saxon-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"European cuisine","img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/a/a7/Borscht_served.jpg/500px-Borscht_served.jpg","w":500,"h":327,"by":"liz west from Boxborough, MA","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Borscht_served.jpg","of":"Borscht"}},"cuisine/french-polynesia-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/french-polynesia-cuisine","n":"French Polynesian cuisine","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in France","intro":[["French Polynesia is an overseas collectivity of France and its sole overseas country; it is classified as a non-self-governing territory by the United Nations. It comprises 121 geographically dispersed islands and atolls stretching over more than 2,000 kilometres (1,200 mi) in the South Pacific Ocean. French Polynesia is associated with the European Union as an overseas country and territory (OCT). The total land area of French Polynesia is 3,521 square kilometres (1,359 sq mi), with a population of 282,596 as of September 2025 of which at least 205,000 live in the Society Islands and the remaining population lives in the rest of the archipelago."],["French Polynesia is divided into five island groups: the Austral Islands; the Gambier Islands; the Marquesas Islands; the Society Islands (comprising the Leeward and Windward Islands); and the Tuamotus. Among its 121 islands and atolls, 75 were inhabited at the 2017 census. Tahiti, which is in the Society Islands group, is the most populous island, home to nearly 69% of the population of French Polynesia as of 2017. Papeete, located on Tahiti, is the capital of French Polynesia. Although not an integral part of its territory, Clipperton Island was administered from French Polynesia until 2007."],["Hundreds of years after the Great Polynesian Migration, European explorers began traveling through the region, visiting the islands of French Polynesia on several occasions. Traders and whaling ships also visited. In 1842, the French took over the islands and established a French protectorate that they called Établissements français d'Océanie."],["In 1946, the EFO became an overseas territory under the constitution of the French Fourth Republic, and Polynesians were granted the right to vote through citizenship. In 1957, the territory was renamed French Polynesia. In 1983, it became a member of the Pacific Community, a regional development organization. Since 28 March 2003, French Polynesia has been an overseas collectivity of the French Republic under the constitutional revision of article 74, and later gained, with law 2004-192 of 27 February 2004, an administrative autonomy, two symbolic manifestations of which are the title of the President of French Polynesia and its additional designation as an overseas country."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"french-polynesia-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/French_Polynesia#Cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[]}],"cont":"European cuisine","img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/8/8d/Archipel_des_Tuamotu.jpg/500px-Archipel_des_Tuamotu.jpg","w":500,"h":341,"by":"Jeff Schmaltz","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Archipel_des_Tuamotu.jpg"}},"ingredient/bacon":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/bacon","n":"Bacon","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 15 dishes across 21 cuisines","intro":[["Bacon is a type of salt-cured pork made from various cuts, typically the belly or less fatty parts of the back. It is eaten as a side dish (particularly in breakfasts), used as a central ingredient (e.g., the BLT sandwich), or as a flavouring or accent."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-bacon"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bacon"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":21,"groups":[["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine",[["Club sandwich","dish/club-sandwich"],["Cobb salad"