 nem dishes. The term rice paper wrappers can sometimes be a misnomer, as some banh trang wrappers are made from rice flour supplemented with tapioca flour or sometimes replaced completely with tapioca starch. The roasted version is bánh tráng nướng."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-banh-trang"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/B%C3%A1nh_tr%C3%A1ng"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Vietnamese cuisine","cuisine/vietnamese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":6,"links":[["Maize","ingredient/maize"],["Mungbean","ingredient/mungbean"],["Potato","ingredient/potato"],["Rice flour","ingredient/rice-flour"],["Sweet potato","ingredient/sweet-potato"],["Tapioca","ingredient/tapioca"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Viet World Kitchen","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"vietworldkitchen.com","note":"Andrea Nguyen's blog on Vietnamese cooking, ingredients and technique.","url":"https://vietworldkitchen.com/blog/category/recipes-vietnamese","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Hungry Huy","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"hungryhuy.com","note":"Vietnamese-American Huy Vu's recipes for home-style Vietnamese dishes.","url":"https://www.hungryhuy.com/vietnamese/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Vietnamese cuisine","links":[["Bánh bao","dish/banh-bao"],["Bánh bột lọc","dish/banh-bot-loc"],["Bánh giầy","dish/banh-giay"],["Chả rươi","dish/cha-ruoi"],["Chả trứng","dish/cha-trung"],["Chạo tôm","dish/chao-tom"],["Coconut candy","dish/coconut-candy"],["Cốm","dish/com"],["Khô cá lóc","dish/kho-ca-loc"],["Mắm nêm","dish/mam-nem"],["Nước chấm","dish/nuoc-cham"],["Ốc vú nàng","dish/oc-vu-nang"],["Shaking beef","dish/shaking-beef"],["Tương","dish/tuong"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/a/a5/Nems_wafer_DSC04723.JPG/500px-Nems_wafer_DSC04723.JPG","w":500,"h":484,"by":"David Monniaux","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nems_wafer_DSC04723.JPG"}},"ingredient/fruit-juice":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/fruit-juice","n":"Fruit juice","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Juice is a drink made from the extraction or pressing of the natural liquid contained in fruit and vegetables. It can also refer to liquids that are flavored with concentrate or other biological food sources like meat or seafood, such as clam juice. Juice is commonly consumed as a beverage or used as an ingredient or flavoring in foods or other beverages, such as smoothies. Juice emerged as a popular beverage choice after the development of pasteurization methods enabled its preservation without using fermentation (which is used in wine production). The largest fruit juice consumers are New Zealand (nearly a cup, or 8 ounces, each day) and Colombia (more than three quarters of a cup each day). Fruit juice consumption on average increases with a country's income level."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-fruit-juice"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fruit_juice"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine",[["Cassatella di sant'Agata","dish/cassatella-di-sant-agata"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Candied peel","ingredient/candied-peel"],["Ceramic glaze","ingredient/ceramic-glaze"],["Icing","ingredient/icing"],["Liqueur","ingredient/liqueur"],["Marzipan","ingredient/marzipan"],["Ricotta","ingredient/ricotta"],["Sponge cake","ingredient/sponge-cake"],["Succade","ingredient/succade"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/f/fd/Orange_juice_1.jpg/500px-Orange_juice_1.jpg","w":500,"h":711,"by":"Agricultural Research Service","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Orange_juice_1.jpg"}},"ingredient/palm-oil":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/palm-oil","n":"Palm oil","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 6 dishes across 3 cuisines","intro":[["Palm oil is an edible vegetable oil derived from the reddish mesocarp (pulp) of the fruit of the African oil palm. The oil is used in food manufacturing, in beauty products, and as biofuel. Palm oil accounted for about 36% of global oils produced from oil crops in 2017. Palm oils are easier to stabilize and maintain quality of flavor and consistency in ultra-processed foods, so they are frequently favored by food manufacturers.  Globally, humans consumed an average of 7.7 kg (17 lb) of palm oil per person in 2015. Demand has also increased for other uses, such as cosmetics and biofuels, encouraging the growth of palm oil plantations in tropical countries."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-palm-oil"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Palm_oil"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":3,"groups":[["Nigerian cuisine","cuisine/nigerian-cuisine",[["Edikang ikong","dish/edikang-ikong"],["Edo black soup","dish/edo-black-soup"],["Miyar zogale","dish/miyar-zogale"],["Moin moin","dish/moin-moin"]]],["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Rawon","dish/rawon"]]],["Yoruba cuisine","cuisine/yoruba-cuisine",[["Draw soup","dish/draw-soup"]]]]},{"h":"Where it's grown","grown":{"what":"Palm oil","years":"2020 to 2024","url":"https://www.fao.org/faostat/en/#data/QCL","world":78871248,"top":[["Indonesia","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",47093366],["Malaysia","cuisine/malaysian-cuisine",18565584],["Thailand","cuisine/thai-cuisine",3048300],["Colombia","cuisine/colombian-cuisine",1728818],["Nigeria","cuisine/nigerian-cuisine",1377500],["Guatemala","cuisine/guatemalan-cuisine",923616],["Papua New Guinea","cuisine/papua-new-guinean-cuisine",756250],["Honduras","cuisine/honduran-cuisine",662500],["Brazil","cuisine/brazilian-cuisine",593550],["Côte d'Ivoire","cuisine/ivorian-cuisine",554944],["Ecuador","cuisine/ecuadorian-cuisine",468436],["Mexico","cuisine/mexican-cuisine",340900],["Cameroon","cuisine/cameroonian-cuisine",333366],["Dem. Rep. Congo","cuisine/congolese-cuisine",300000],["Ghana","cuisine/ghanaian-cuisine",285620]]}},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Crayfish","ingredient/crayfish"],["Fish","ingredient/fish"],["Peppercorn","ingredient/peppercorn"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Cayenne pepper","ingredient/cayenne-pepper"],["Ewedu","ingredient/ewedu"],["Iru","ingredient/iru"],["Ogbono","ingredient/ogbono"],["Okra","ingredient/okra"],["Catharanthus roseus","ingredient/catharanthus-roseus"],["Pumpkin","ingredient/pumpkin"],["Waterleaf","ingredient/waterleaf"],["Palm fruit juice","ingredient/palm-fruit-juice"],["Peanut","ingredient/peanut"],["Tomato","ingredient/tomato"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/4/4a/Palm_oil.jpg/500px-Palm_oil.jpg","w":500,"h":462,"by":"© Achim Raschka / CC BY-SA 4.0 (via Wikimedia Commons)","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Palm_oil.jpg"}},"ingredient/seedling":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/seedling","n":"Seedling","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 3 dishes across 2 cuisines","intro":[["A seedling is a young sporophyte developing out of a plant embryo from a seed. Seedling development starts with germination of the seed. A typical young seedling consists of three main parts: the radicle (embryonic root), the hypocotyl (embryonic shoot), and the cotyledons (seed leaves). The two classes of flowering plants (angiosperms) are distinguished by their numbers of seed leaves: monocotyledons (monocots) have one blade-shaped cotyledon, whereas dicotyledons (dicots) possess two round cotyledons. Gymnosperms are more varied. For example, pine seedlings have up to eight cotyledons.  The seedlings of some flowering plants have no cotyledons at all. These are said to be acotyledons."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-seedling"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seedling"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Gado-gado","dish/gado-gado"],["Laksa","dish/laksa"],["Mie caluk","dish/mie-caluk"]]],["Acehnese cuisine","cuisine/acehnese-cuisine",[["Mie caluk","dish/mie-caluk"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Cucumber","ingredient/cucumber"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Krupuk","ingredient/krupuk"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Ginger","ingredient/ginger"],["Kencur","ingredient/kencur"],["Ghost pepper","ingredient/ghost-pepper"],["Ipomoea aquatica","ingredient/ipomoea-aquatica"],["Lontong","ingredient/lontong"],["Palm sugar","ingredient/palm-sugar"],["Peanut sauce","ingredient/peanut-sauce"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Rice cake","ingredient/rice-cake"],["Tamarind","ingredient/tamarind"],["Tempeh","ingredient/tempeh"],["Tofu","ingredient/tofu"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/0/01/Monocot_vs_dicot_crop_Pengo.jpg","w":500,"h":766,"by":"w:User:Pengo","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monocot_vs_dicot_crop_Pengo.jpg"}},"ingredient/sheep-milk":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/sheep-milk","n":"Sheep milk","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 5 dishes across 11 cuisines","intro":[["Sheep milk is the milk of domestic sheep. It is commonly used to make cultured dairy products, such as cheese. Some of the most popular sheep cheeses include feta (Greece), pecorino romano (Italy), Roquefort (France) and Manchego (Spain)."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-sheep-milk"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sheep_milk"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":11,"groups":